          {
            "id": "D",
            "text": "$(120,0.6,0.033)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          }
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Extract all three factors, retaining the decimal scale.",
        "source_solution": "Task: evaluate nine products under an arithmetic square root.\nThe root is the nonnegative product of the individual nonnegative roots.\na: 10*7=70.\nb: 11*8=88.\nc: 9*20=180.\nd: 0.6*7=4.2.\ne: 12*0.5=6.\nf: 1.5*0.2=0.3.\ng: 3*8*5=120.\nh: 0.4*0.05*30=0.6.\ni: 1.1*0.3*0.1=0.033.\nDistinction: extracting every factor, including decimal scales, not distributing\nover addition or retaining squared factors.",
        "unit_reason": "A full three-factor result tests the theorem's extension and small decimal magnitudes.",
        "concept_blocks": [
          2,
          9
        ],
        "limitations": [
          "Recognition does not establish independently written multi-factor calculations."
        ],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": [
          "D"
        ],
        "rationales": [
          {
            "option_id": "A",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Add the three extracted roots: 3+8+5, 0.4+0.05+30, 1.1+0.3+0.1.",
            "misconception": "Adding instead of multiplying the roots."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "B",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Leave the last factor unrooted: 3*8*25, 0.4*0.05*900, 1.1*0.3*0.01.",
            "misconception": "Applying extraction to only two of the three factors."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "C",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Use 0.5 instead of sqrt(0.0025)=0.05, and 1 instead of sqrt(0.01)=0.1.",
            "misconception": "Losing a decimal place in the two small-factor cases."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "D",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "3*8*5=120; 0.4*0.05*30=0.6; 1.1*0.3*0.1=0.033.",
            "misconception": null
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q04",
      "source_numbers": [
        "778"
      ],
      "view": {
        "mode": "single",
        "stem": "Evaluate $\\sqrt{16/25}$, $\\sqrt{9/64}$, $\\sqrt{64/49}$ and $\\sqrt{121/25}$, in that order.",
        "figure_ids": [],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "text": "$(4/25,3/64,8/49,11/25)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "text": "$(4/5,3/8,8/7,11/5)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "text": "$(5/4,8/3,7/8,5/11)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "text": "$(16/5,9/8,64/7,121/5)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          }
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Evaluate four fraction roots with numerator and denominator squares.",
        "source_solution": "Task: evaluate eight rational arithmetic square roots.\na: sqrt(16/25)=4/5.\nb: sqrt(9/64)=3/8.\nc: sqrt(64/49)=8/7.\nd: sqrt(121/25)=11/5.\ne: sqrt(144/169)=12/13.\nf: sqrt(225/256)=15/16.\ng: 1 9/16=25/16, hence 5/4.\nh: 2 7/81=169/81, hence 13/9.\nDistinction: root both numerator and denominator; first convert mixed numbers.",
        "unit_reason": "One ordered quartet preserves proper and improper fraction cases, including which side of the quotient each root belongs to.",
        "concept_blocks": [
          11
        ],
        "limitations": [
          "Recognition of a fraction list is not production of the quotient derivations."
        ],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": [
          "B"
        ],
        "rationales": [
          {
            "option_id": "A",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Root each numerator but leave each denominator unchanged.",
            "misconception": "Extracting only the numerator."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "B",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "Root both sides of each positive quotient: 4/5, 3/8, 8/7, 11/5.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "C",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Reverse each quotient of roots.",
            "misconception": "Interchanging numerator and denominator."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "D",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Root only the denominator in each fraction.",
            "misconception": "Leaving numerator squares unextracted."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q05",
      "source_numbers": [
        "778"
      ],
      "view": {
        "mode": "single",
        "stem": "Evaluate $\\sqrt{144/169}$, $\\sqrt{225/256}$, $\\sqrt{1\\frac{9}{16}}$ and $\\sqrt{2\\frac{7}{81}}$, in that order.",
        "figure_ids": [],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
