            "text": "$(12/13,15/16,3/4,\\sqrt{7}/9)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          }
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Evaluate two fraction roots and two mixed-number roots.",
        "source_solution": "Task: evaluate eight rational arithmetic square roots.\na: sqrt(16/25)=4/5.\nb: sqrt(9/64)=3/8.\nc: sqrt(64/49)=8/7.\nd: sqrt(121/25)=11/5.\ne: sqrt(144/169)=12/13.\nf: sqrt(225/256)=15/16.\ng: 1 9/16=25/16, hence 5/4.\nh: 2 7/81=169/81, hence 13/9.\nDistinction: root both numerator and denominator; first convert mixed numbers.",
        "unit_reason": "The full quartet keeps the transition from fractions to mixed numbers, instead of accepting a single converted cell.",
        "concept_blocks": [
          11
        ],
        "limitations": [
          "Recognition does not demonstrate independently converting mixed numbers or writing roots."
        ],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": [
          "A"
        ],
        "rationales": [
          {
            "option_id": "A",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "The first two give 12/13 and 15/16. Mixed numbers are 25/16 and 169/81, whose roots are 5/4 and 13/9.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "B",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Root the numerator of each improper fraction but not its denominator.",
            "misconception": "Keeping denominator squares after extraction."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "C",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Take reciprocals of the correctly converted quotient roots.",
            "misconception": "Reversing the quotient direction."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "D",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Discard the whole-number part before rooting 9/16 and 7/81.",
            "misconception": "Reading a mixed number as only its fractional part."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q06",
      "source_numbers": [
        "779"
      ],
      "view": {
        "mode": "single",
        "stem": "Evaluate $\\sqrt{810\\times40}$, $\\sqrt{72\\times32}$ and $\\sqrt{75\\times48}$, in that order.",
        "figure_ids": [],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "text": "$(90,24,30)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "text": "$(32400,2304,3600)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "text": "$(180,48,60)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "text": "$(29,19,17)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          }
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Regroup nonsquare integer factors into square products.",
        "source_solution": "Task: factor non-obvious products into squares and evaluate six roots.\na: 810*40=81*400=32400, root 180.\nb: 72*32=9*256=2304, root 48.\nc: 75*48=25*144=3600, root 60.\nd: 4.9*360=49*36=1764, root 42.\ne: 2.5*14.4=25*1.44=36, root 6.\nf: 90*6.4=9*64=576, root 24.\nDistinction: regroup factors without changing the product or decimal magnitude.",
        "unit_reason": "Three integer regroupings are one complete ordered calculation unit, unlike the already-square factors in q01.",
        "concept_blocks": [
          2
        ],
        "limitations": [
          "Result recognition does not prove independently finding the square factorizations."
        ],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": [
          "C"
        ],
        "rationales": [
          {
            "option_id": "A",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "The radicands factor as 2^4*3^4*5^2, 2^8*3^2 and 2^4*3^2*5^2. Extract one fewer factor 2 than the paired powers supply: 2*9*5=90, 2^3*3=24, 2*3*5=30.",
            "misconception": "Omitting one extracted factor 2 from the prime-square pairing."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "B",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Compute the three products but stop without taking their roots.",
            "misconception": "Reporting the radicand as the square root."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "C",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "81*400, 9*256 and 25*144 have roots 9*20, 3*16 and 5*12.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "D",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "After valid square regrouping, add roots 9+20, 3+16 and 5+12.",
            "misconception": "Turning a product of extracted factors into a sum."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
