        "task": "Regroup decimal products without changing their magnitude.",
        "source_solution": "Task: factor non-obvious products into squares and evaluate six roots.\na: 810*40=81*400=32400, root 180.\nb: 72*32=9*256=2304, root 48.\nc: 75*48=25*144=3600, root 60.\nd: 4.9*360=49*36=1764, root 42.\ne: 2.5*14.4=25*1.44=36, root 6.\nf: 90*6.4=9*64=576, root 24.\nDistinction: regroup factors without changing the product or decimal magnitude.",
        "unit_reason": "The three regroupings test decimal compensation across two factors as a complete result.",
        "concept_blocks": [
          2
        ],
        "limitations": [
          "Recognition does not demonstrate written decimal regrouping."
        ],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": [
          "B"
        ],
        "rationales": [
          {
            "option_id": "A",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Use valid regroupings 49*36, 25*1.44, 9*64 but add their roots 7+6, 5+1.2, 3+8.",
            "misconception": "Adding extracted factors."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "B",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "The products equal 49*36, 25*1.44 and 9*64, giving 42, 6 and 24.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "C",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Move decimal points to obtain 49*3600, 25*144, 900*64; each product becomes 100 times too large.",
            "misconception": "Moving both decimal scales in the same direction instead of compensating."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "D",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Multiply the factors and report the products without root extraction.",
            "misconception": "Stopping at the radicand."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q08",
      "source_numbers": [
        "780"
      ],
      "view": {
        "mode": "multiple",
        "stem": "Select every valid argument establishing either $\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}<3+4$ or $\\sqrt{10^2-6^2}>10-6$.",
        "figure_ids": [],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "text": "$\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=\\sqrt{25}=5$, while $3+4=7$; therefore $5<7$.",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "text": "$\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=3+4=7$; therefore the first comparison is an equality.",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "text": "$\\sqrt{10^2-6^2}=10-6=4$; therefore the second comparison is an equality.",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "text": "$\\sqrt{10^2-6^2}=\\sqrt{64}=8$, while $10-6=4$; therefore $8>4$.",
            "figure_ids": []
          }
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Judge valid proofs of both strict root comparisons.",
        "source_solution": "Task: prove each of two strict inequalities.\na: 3^2+4^2=25, sqrt(25)=5<7=3+4.\nb: 10^2-6^2=64, sqrt(64)=8>4=10-6.\nBoth comparisons use explicitly nonnegative arithmetic roots.\nDistinction: a root does not distribute over a sum or difference.",
        "unit_reason": "Select all valid complete arguments; the two independent inequalities are evaluated in one native multiple response rather than coded combinations.",
        "concept_blocks": [],
        "limitations": [
          "Recognizing valid arguments does not demonstrate independently composing either proof."
        ],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": [
          "A",
          "D"
        ],
        "rationales": [
          {
            "option_id": "A",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "9+16=25, its arithmetic root is 5, and 5<7.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "B",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Distribute the root across addition to obtain the invalid equality 7.",
            "misconception": "Assuming square roots distribute over addition."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "C",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Distribute the root across subtraction to obtain the invalid equality 4.",
            "misconception": "Assuming square roots distribute over subtraction."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "D",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "100-36=64, its arithmetic root is 8, and 8>4.",
            "misconception": null
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q09",
      "source_numbers": [
        "781"
      ],
      "view": {
        "mode": "single",
        "stem": "Evaluate $\\sqrt{13^2-12^2}$, $\\sqrt{313^2-312^2}$, $\\sqrt{17^2-8^2}$ and $\\sqrt{117^2-108^2}$, in that order.",
        "figure_ids": [],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "text": "$(1,1,9,9)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "text": "$(25,625,225,2025)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "text": "$(\\sqrt{25},\\sqrt{625},\\sqrt{25},\\sqrt{225})$",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "text": "$(5,25,15,45)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          }
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Calculate four difference-of-squares roots.",
        "source_solution": "Task: calculate four roots of differences of squares.\na: (13-12)(13+12)=25, root 5.\nb: (313-312)(313+312)=625, root 25.\nc: (17-8)(17+8)=225, root 15.\nd: (117-108)(117+108)=2025, root 45.\nDistinction: difference-of-squares factoring, not taking the difference of roots.",
        "unit_reason": "A complete quartet retains adjacent-number and larger-gap differences; each requires both factors of the difference-of-squares identity.",
        "concept_blocks": [
          2
        ],
        "limitations": [
          "Selecting values does not prove independently writing the factorization."
        ],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": [
          "D"
        ],
        "rationales": [
          {
            "option_id": "A",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Subtract the unsquared bases directly: 13-12, 313-312, 17-8, 117-108.",
            "misconception": "Distributing the radical over subtraction."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "B",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Compute each difference of squares but do not extract its root.",
            "misconception": "Reporting squared results."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "C",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Keep only the sum factor: use sqrt(u+v) instead of sqrt((u-v)(u+v)). The first two coincide because their difference factor is 1; the last two miss factor 9.",
            "misconception": "Omitting the difference factor in the factorization."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "D",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "The radicands factor to 1*25, 1*625, 9*25 and 9*225, giving 5,25,15,45.",
            "misconception": null
          }
        ]
      }
    },
