      "intent": {
        "task": "Rationalize four single-radical denominators while preserving signs.",
        "source_solution": "Printed source label: 737. Task: write eight fractions in a*sqrt(b) form.\nRationalize with the denominator radical; all radicands here are positive integers.\na: 5/sqrt(10)=5sqrt(10)/10=(1/2)sqrt(10).\nb: 7/sqrt(5)=(7/5)sqrt(5).\nc: -6/sqrt(2)=-6sqrt(2)/2=-3sqrt(2).\nd: -12/sqrt(3)=-12sqrt(3)/3=-4sqrt(3).\ne: 5/(2sqrt(3))=5sqrt(3)/6.\nf: 21/(5sqrt(14))=21sqrt(14)/70=(3/10)sqrt(14).\ng: -3sqrt(5)/sqrt(2)=-3sqrt(10)/2.\nh: -7sqrt(6)/sqrt(3)=-7sqrt(2).\nThe same letter a in the requested target is merely a rational coefficient, not\nthe variable a in unrelated source tasks. No N-zero convention is required.\nDistinction: multiply numerator and denominator equally and preserve leading signs.",
        "unit_reason": "The complete quartet compares rational coefficients and two negative numerators in the requested a*sqrt(b) form.",
        "concept_blocks": [
          11
        ],
        "limitations": [
          "Recognition does not prove independently multiplying numerator and denominator by the same radical."
        ],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": [
          "C"
        ],
        "rationales": [
          {
            "option_id": "A",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Multiply each numerator by the denominator radical but fail to divide by its squared radicand.",
            "misconception": "Multiplying only the numerator when rationalizing."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "B",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Rationalize but drop the leading minus signs in the last two entries.",
            "misconception": "Losing a negative numerator through root manipulation."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "C",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "Coefficients 5/10=1/2,7/5,-6/2=-3,-12/3=-4 preserve all signs.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "D",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "After correct rationalization, divide the coefficient by the radicand once again: 10,5,2,3.",
            "misconception": "Counting the rationalized denominator twice."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
