        "source_solution": "Task: six roots using a squares table and a scaled-square derivation format.\na: 448900=4489*100=67^2*10^2, root 670.\nb: 115600=1156*100=34^2*10^2, root 340.\nc: 7840000=784*10000=28^2*100^2, root 2800.\nd: 51.84=5184/100=72^2/10^2, root 7.2.\ne: 62.41=6241/100=79^2/10^2, root 7.9.\nf: 0.7744=7744/10000=88^2/100^2, root 0.88.\nSquare checks: 67^2=4489, 34^2=1156, 28^2=784, 72^2=5184,\n79^2=6241, 88^2=7744. No back-of-book file was opened.\nLoss: choice recognition and independent square verification do not prove table\nlookup or production of the displayed derivation.",
        "unit_reason": "A complete three-derivation result retains denominator extraction and two distinct decimal scales.",
        "concept_blocks": [
          11
        ],
        "limitations": [
          "No back-of-book squares table is consulted; table-lookup performance is not measured.",
          "Selecting derivations does not demonstrate independently writing them."
        ],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": [
          "B"
        ],
        "rationales": [
          {
            "option_id": "A",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Take numerator roots while leaving the denominator scales squared.",
            "misconception": "Extracting only the numerator of a scaled quotient."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "B",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "72^2=5184,79^2=6241,88^2=7744; denominators are 100,100,10000 and their roots 10,10,100.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "C",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Discard the denominator power-of-ten factors entirely.",
            "misconception": "Losing the decimal scaling denominator."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "D",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Replace division of extracted roots by subtraction.",
            "misconception": "Confusing a quotient with a difference; it also violates nonnegativity in the last case."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
