            "text": "$(3a,5b,-0.6x,-9y^3)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "text": "$(9a,25b,-0.36x,-81y^3)$",
            "figure_ids": []
          }
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Simplify four square radicals under their explicit sign conditions.",
        "source_solution": "Task: simplify four radicals to monomials under the given sign conditions.\na: sqrt(9a^2)=3|a|=3a since a>=0.\nb: sqrt(25b^2)=5|b|=5b since b>0.\nc: sqrt(0.36x^2)=0.6|x|=-0.6x since x<0.\nd: sqrt(81y^6)=9|y^3|=-9y^3 since y<=0.\nAt y=0 the last value is 0. Negative inputs must still yield nonnegative roots.\nDistinction: arithmetic square root of a square is absolute value.",
        "unit_reason": "The entire monomial list contrasts positive, negative and zero-allowed inputs; the conditions control the signs collectively.",
        "concept_blocks": [
          2
        ],
        "limitations": [
          "Selecting monomials does not prove independently writing absolute-value reductions."
        ],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": [
          "C"
        ],
        "rationales": [
          {
            "option_id": "A",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Use sqrt(z^2)=z without absolute value; last entries are negative for negative inputs.",
            "misconception": "Ignoring the sign-dependent absolute value."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "B",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Use the negative branch for every input, making the first two roots negative when a,b>0.",
            "misconception": "Treating an arithmetic square root as the negative solution of a square equation."
          },
          {
            "option_id": "C",
            "truth": "correct",
            "derivation": "Use 3|a|,5|b|,0.6|x|,9|y^3| and the four given signs; y=0 remains valid.",
            "misconception": null
          },
          {
            "option_id": "D",
            "truth": "incorrect",
            "derivation": "Resolve variable signs but leave numerical square factors unchanged.",
            "misconception": "Taking roots of variable powers but not numerical coefficients."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
